{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of exact clean head 2808a1289d0cfac9abcdaac7a54b52eceeb9e43a on request/merge-staleness-docs, for review request dff2f74e under promise 1ced0eda, co-signing artifacts 42c44ff0 at docs and 5392b555 at internal/workroom.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are the documentation set and internal/workroom. Both contracts are preserved, and the head contains no contract change: the internal/workroom change is one comment plus two reflowed lines with no code touched, and the three documentation pages correct claims about cmd/gs behaviour without asserting any new behaviour. docs/reference/architecture.md therefore needs no change in this head, and a change there would itself have been the finding. The one thing I checked rather than assumed is that the corrected prose describes the layer it belongs to: merge.md and staleness.md describe cmd/gs, which is the layer that owns the merge gate, and neither page now claims the fold enforces it.\n\nSECURITY. Nothing executable changes, so there is no new trust boundary, input path, signature check or failure mode. The question that matters here is the other one the request asked: could a corrected sentence mislead an operator into a less safe act than the wrong sentence did? I judge not, and it is worth saying why, because the change moves prose in the permissive direction. The old text said merge refuses a stale approval, which was falsely reassuring — an operator reading it would believe the tool would stop them, and the tool would not. The new text says the merge lands the stale head and records what moved, and it names the two facts that do refuse. That replaces a false guarantee with a true one and tells the reader where the real boundary is. merge.md's new section also states the remedy for the refusing case — a fresh artifact on current bases and a fresh review, not another verdict on the same chain — which is the operationally important half and was absent before.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The new merge.md section earns its length. It is fourteen lines replacing five, and the added lines are not restatement: they name the receipt mechanism, separate the two refusing facts from ordinary staleness, and give the remedy. I looked for a cut and did not find one that keeps the meaning. One observation rather than a change request: staleness.md, work-loop.md and merge.md now each carry a version of the same distinction, which is duplication the repository generally dislikes. It is justified here because the three pages serve different readers and each already carried the wrong claim in its own words, so leaving any one of them pointing at another would have left a wrong sentence standing. I would not cut any of the three now, but if a fourth page ever needs it, that is the moment to make merge.md canonical and link to it.\n\nTHE FIVE NAMED CHECKS. I verified each against the code at the exact head, not against the request text.\n\nOne, the mechanisms. CONFIRMED. mergeStalenessTrailer is \"Gitseq-Staleness: \" at cmd/gs/main.go:590 and mergeReceiptMessage appends it at :614-615. The durable assertion sets stale to \"true\" and staleness at cmd/gs/succession.go:458-460. Both are conditional on staleness being non-empty, which is what \"writes what had moved\" should mean, so the prose is right; a reader will not expect an empty trailer when nothing moved.\n\nTwo, the ineffective row. CONFIRMED, and you read the right function. standingArtifact at cmd/gs/main.go:1000-1002 returns \"artifact is not effective\" from decisionEffective, and \"artifact is retired\" at :1005-1007; liveArtifact at :1016-1025 adds \"artifact describes a superseded world\". The three refusals in your table row are exactly those three. There is a fourth, \"artifact event is unknown\" at :1011, which I am not asking you to add: an unknown event is not a state an approved artifact can be in.\n\nThree, the co-signed set. CONFIRMED, and the rule really is identical rather than merely similar, because it is the same function. The primary goes through liveArtifact at cmd/gs/main.go:897; validateReviewedSet calls liveArtifact on every member at cmd/gs/succession.go:551. Your sentence is careful to say \"the same staleness rule\", which is what I can confirm. The set members additionally must stand at the reviewed head (:553-555), and the primary has its own equivalent at main.go:901-903, so the two are symmetric there too. Two line numbers in your request text drifted — validateReviewedSet begins at :543 and its liveArtifact call is at :551, not :541 — but no line number appears in the shipped prose, so nothing to fix in the head.\n\nFour, the fold.go comment. NO REGRESSION. The mechanism does not now read as historical. The tense change is confined to the motivating paragraph, which was already past tense in every other clause, and the paragraph immediately below it — \"Only a genuine successor may borrow the plan, and only its own. Citing a receipt is not enough\" — states the live rule in the present and is untouched. A reader reaches the operative rule in present tense directly after the history. Adding \"then\" is the minimum edit that removes the false present-tense claim, which is the right size for a comment change riding along with a documentation repair.\n\nFive, completeness. I searched independently rather than repeating your greps, sweeping docs/, AGENTS.md, SKILL.md and README.md for every line containing \"stale\" together with any of merge, refus, block, strict or reject. NO FIFTH WRONG PASSAGE REMAINS. Everything the sweep returned is either this head's own corrected text or already accurate: SKILL.md:193-195 already says merge records ordinary staleness and refuses a world-stale artifact or approval; AGENTS.md:251 already says the same; work-loop.md:222 is about gs review, which genuinely does not refuse; docs/reference/mcp/status.md:88 says staleness blocks nothing; merge.md:160 and :171 are about succession and are correct.\n\nOne near-miss is worth recording because the next auditor will hit it. docs/concepts/connectors.md:81-85 says \"It must be live. Retirement withdraws an admission and stops it at once. Staleness refuses too.\" That reads like a fifth instance and is not one: it describes connector clause admission, a different door, and it is true there — internal/connector/github/read.go:71 says \"Retired and stale clauses are both refused, and this fails closed on purpose\", refusing Retired at :102 and Stale at :106. Leave it alone. The two doors genuinely differ, and that is the point worth remembering: staleness is permissive at the merge gate and fail-closed at connector admission.\n\nGATES. Re-run by me at this exact head rather than trusted. go vet ./... exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; go test -count=1 ./... exit 0 with all 27 packages ok, including internal/docset in 101.3s, cmd/gs in 181.8s and internal/kernel in 174.9s. The worktree at /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/merge-staleness-docs is clean, git status --porcelain empty, HEAD exactly 2808a128. The head fast-forwards from main 92125957 and the diff is the four files claimed, +45/-23.\n\nBoth artifacts are live, unretired, standing at this exact head, each with exactly one live predecessor — b712c16c at docs and 16dd3fd1 at internal/workroom — so the merge receipt has a clean single predecessor to retire at each path. Any change to this head invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:42c44ff0ce6ccf9f9bcd148ab5c59e905317e647","head":"2808a1289d0cfac9abcdaac7a54b52eceeb9e43a","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2ae7a20da3c99adf13e3bb1606c7aa344350e7be, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:310c4f33f1b8e093c065424c8a4fb3f5fc8b388c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5b673fdc0edaf6659171d74fd89486c2bfade9ec